      "text": "The problem provides two vector equations representing two linear transformations for the matrix $\\mathbf{M}.$ The first equation gives us:\n\n\\begin{align*} \n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ 21 \\end{pmatrix} & \\implies 3\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{bmatrix} 6 \\\\ 21 \\end{bmatrix} \\\\[5pt] \n\\implies \\mathbf{M} = \\frac{1}{3} \\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\\\ 7 \\end{bmatrix} & \\text{dividing by 3 on both sides of the equation}\\\\[5pt] \n\\implies \\mathbf{M} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} \\end{bmatrix}. \n\\end{align*}\n\nThe second equation provides another linear transformation:\n\n\\begin{align*} \n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -17 \\end{pmatrix} & \\implies -\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{bmatrix} 3 \\\\ -17 \\end{bmatrix} \\\\[5pt] \n\\implies \\mathbf{M} = \\begin{bmatrix} -3 \\\\ 17 \\end{bmatrix}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nWith these two last two equations, we now have a system to solve:\n\n\\begin{align*} \n\\mathbf{M} &\\stackrel{(1)}{=} \\begin{bmatrix} \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} \\end{bmatrix} \\\\[5pt] \n\\mathbf{M} &\\stackrel{(2)}{=} \\begin{bmatrix} -3 \\\\ 17 \\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{align*}\n\nThe problem is asking for two matrices, $\\mathbf{A}$ and $\\mathbf{B},$ where $\\mathbf{A} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{B} \\begin{pmatrix} -x \\\\ x \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} b \\\\ -a \\end{pmatrix}.$ We can find these matrices using the components:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{A} &\\stackrel{(3)}{=} \\frac{1}{2} - 3y \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{2} + \\begin{pmatrix} -3y \\\\ -3y \\end{pmatrix}, \\\\[5pt]\n\\mathbf{B} &\\stackrel{(4)}{=} \\mathbf{M}  \\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -7 \\\\ -7 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\end{align*}\n\n\nNumber of triangles:\nGiven a right triangle ABC with sides AB = 5 cm, BC = 12 cm, and AC = 13 cm, we use the Pythagorean theorem:\n{eq}BC^2 = AB^2 + AC^2 {(3.1)} {/eq}\nProceeding, we find:\nNext, we calculate the measure of angle A in triangle ABC:\n{eq}arctan \\left(\\frac{AC}{BC}\\right) {/eq}\nTo find the measure of angle A, apply the inverse tangent function:\nAngle A = arctan (12/5)\nTherefore, the angle A is:\nAngle A ≈ 67.38°\n$$\\boxed{67.38^\\circ}$$\nThis is the measure of angle A in triangle ABC.",
